STIMULI = [
    {"id": "qd-t1-a", "title": "Pollinating date palms", "text":
     "Date palms grow as separate male and female trees, and only the female trees bear fruit. In the wild, wind and insects carry pollen from one to the other, but the results are uneven: many flowers are never pollinated, and the harvest suffers. For this reason, growers in oases have long done the work themselves. When the flowers open in spring, a worker climbs each female tree and ties a few strands of male flowers among its blossoms. One male tree can supply enough pollen for dozens of female trees, so a well-run grove needs only a handful of them. This saves space for the trees that matter most to the farmer. The method is slow and tiring, and some modern farms now blow pollen onto the trees with machines. Even so, many growers still prefer the old way, which they trust to set more fruit."},
    {"id": "qd-t1-b", "title": "How paper travelled west", "text":
     "Paper was first made in China, where workers soaked plant fibres, beat them into a pulp and dried thin sheets on screens. For centuries the method stayed in the east. In the eighth century it reached Samarkand, a trading city in Central Asia, and from there it spread quickly through the Islamic world. Baghdad soon had paper mills of its own. Officials there welcomed the new material because it was cheaper than parchment, which was made from animal skin, and harder to alter without leaving a mark. That made it well suited to government records. As paper became common, books grew less costly, and libraries and booksellers multiplied in the cities of the region. Centuries later, papermaking crossed into Europe, where it would one day meet the printing press."},
    {"id": "qd-t1-c", "title": "Fresh water from the sea", "text":
     "Many coastal countries with little rainfall turn seawater into drinking water. The older method is thermal: seawater is heated until it boils, and the steam, which leaves the salt behind, is cooled back into water. It works well but burns a great deal of fuel. Most new plants instead use reverse osmosis, pushing seawater at high pressure through membranes with pores so small that salt cannot pass. This takes far less energy, although the membranes must be cleaned and replaced regularly. Both methods leave behind brine, a very salty liquid that is usually returned to the sea. If it is released in one place, it can harm the plants and animals on the seabed nearby, so engineers spread it through long pipes with many outlets. Cutting this damage is now as important to designers as cutting cost."},
    {"id": "qd-t1-d", "title": "Sleep and memory", "text":
     "For a long time, sleep was thought of as a pause in the brain's work. Research over the past few decades suggests otherwise. In many experiments, people who learned a list of words or a new skill and then slept recalled it better the next day than people who stayed awake for the same number of hours. Scientists believe that during sleep the brain replays some of the day's experiences, strengthening the connections that hold new memories. Not every stage of sleep seems to do the same job, which is one reason a full night appears to matter more than a short nap. None of this means that students can learn while asleep. It does suggest that a night of rest after studying is part of learning, not time taken away from it."},
    {"id": "qd-t1-e", "title": "The camel's hump", "text":
     "A common belief holds that a camel stores water in its hump. In fact, the hump is mostly fat. Keeping fat in one place, rather than spread under the skin, may help the animal lose heat from the rest of its body. The camel's real defences against thirst lie elsewhere. It can lose a large share of its body water and keep working, a loss that would kill most mammals. Its kidneys produce very concentrated urine, and its body temperature is allowed to rise during the day, which reduces sweating. When water is finally found, a camel can drink an enormous amount in minutes and recover quickly."},
    {"id": "qd-t1-f", "title": "The honeybee's dance", "text":
     "When a honeybee finds a rich patch of flowers, she returns to the hive and performs a dance on the honeycomb. In the waggle dance, she runs a short straight line while shaking her body, then circles back and repeats it. The angle of the straight run tells the other bees the direction of the food compared with the position of the sun. The length of the waggle tells them how far away it is. Bees that follow the dance then fly out and search the area it describes. Remarkably, this message is given in the dark interior of the hive, so the followers learn it by touch and vibration rather than by sight."},
    {"id": "qd-t1-g", "title": "Corals of the Red Sea", "text":
     "Around the world, coral reefs are suffering as the oceans warm. When the water stays too hot for too long, corals expel the tiny algae that live inside them and give them food and colour. The coral turns white, an event known as bleaching, and may die if the heat lasts. Some reefs in the northern Red Sea, however, have so far coped with warm water better than many others. One explanation is that their ancestors reached the area through waters at the sea's southern end that are much hotter, so only heat-tolerant corals survived the journey. Scientists study these reefs in the hope of learning how other corals might be helped."},
]

